# Python bytecode / caches
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.coverage
htmlcov/

# Scratch directories whose name starts with "plan", but **not** the plans
# themselves. This was `plan*`, which matched `plan9.md` as well as the
# directories `eval/imitation/`, `eval/rl-single-train/` and
# `data/plan9_step1_backup/`, so every plan after the first was written and then
# invisible to git - and `plan.md` was the only one tracked, purely because it
# predates the rule.
#
# The trailing slash is the whole change: a pattern with one matches files *and*
# directories, a pattern ending in `/` matches directories only. So the plans go
# in and the directories stay out, and - the property that matters here - a
# negation below cannot re-include anything *inside* a `plan*` directory,
# because git does not descend into an excluded directory. There is no
# accidental hole for this to open; `eval/imitation/` and `eval/rl-single-train/`
# are un-ignored on purpose, by their own lines further down.
plan*/

# ...but the evaluation results under `eval/imitation/` and
# `eval/rl-single-train/` are not plans, they are evidence, and an evaluation
# that cannot be recomputed from the repository is not evidence. `plan*` above
# matches any path *component* starting with "plan", so it silently swallowed
# the whole directory - no warning, the files just never appeared in
# `git status`. Two lines bring them back, and the `plan*.md` files it was
# written for stay ignored.
#
# Unlike the `data/` block further down, no three-line dance is needed here: that
# one had to re-include a directory whose *parent* was excluded, and git refuses
# to descend into an excluded parent. `eval/` itself is not excluded, so naming
# the two directories is enough.
!eval/imitation/
!eval/rl-single-train/

# Virtual environments. `.venv-rl/` is the stage-G reinforcement-learning
# environment: about 4 GiB of CUDA wheels, regenerateable with one pip command.
.venv/
.venv-rl/
venv/
env/

# `data/` is regenerable in principle and enormous in practice - the imitation
# shards, the H-B1/H-C2 datasets and every other training checkpoint - so it is
# ignored as a whole, which also covers `data/manifest.json`. Its contents are
# reproduced in `reports/g_report.md`.
#
# **Except the four checkpoints the game can be played against.** A fresh clone
# has to be able to sit down and play, and a seat whose weights are not in the
# repository is not a seat - it is a name that fails to load on first use.
#
# The three lines per directory are not redundant. gitignore cannot re-include a
# file whose parent *directory* the pattern matched, so a single `data/*` plus
# `!data/hc2/step_001000.pt` below it is silently dead: `data/*` matches the
# directory `data/hc2`, and everything under an excluded directory stays
# excluded no matter what follows. The directory has to be un-ignored first,
# then its contents re-excluded, and only then can the one file be named.
#
# plan9a stage 4 published two of these four into `ai/checkpoints/` (verified
# sha256 against the source first), so their negations came out; plan9 task 4
# then retired all six imitation seats and put the 0k originals back under
# version control, which is what the four negations below do. `ai/checkpoints/`
# now holds only the four trained policies, so the only copy of the 0k weights
# that a fresh clone has is `data/` - and `data/` is where the retired-seat
# replays (`match.py --adhoc`) and `rl/`'s own tests read them from. Every one
# of the four was md5-checked against the `ai/checkpoints/` copy it replaces
# before that copy was deleted.
#
# `step_002000.pt` / `step_010000.pt` keep their negations for a different
# reason: those two were never published and are no longer a seat's load path
# either - they are simply the H-C2 weights the evidence cites, and deleting
# them was never on the table.
data/*
!data/hc2/
data/hc2/*
!data/hc2/step_001000.pt
!data/hc2/step_002000.pt
!data/hc2/step_010000.pt
!data/imit_o1000/
data/imit_o1000/*
!data/imit_o1000/step_001000.pt
!data/imit_b1000/
data/imit_b1000/*
!data/imit_b1000/step_001000.pt
!data/imit_i1000/
data/imit_i1000/*
!data/imit_i1000/step_001000.pt
!data/rl1/
data/rl1/*

# Runtime-generated game data (recreated on first play)
records.json
settings.json

